A||N||18||bHooper||Pulley's, belts and water pump.. a new theory.||||5||
Z||000000||bHooper||09-26-2000||07:55 PM||||wed. nite, i lost the 3rd serpentine belt, off my GT, in 3 months.  this one snapped instead of slipping and fraying (it was curled on the edge). (i think i had started a thread about this, b4 i went to indy, but cant remember)<P>today, i had time to take a look at the whle system (pulley, tensioner and altenator).  nothing "wobbles", but the water pump pulley only spins about 1-1 1/2 turns, by hand... with no belt.<P>it also appears that the altenator/tensioner had not moved much (if at all), as the adjustment bolt (underneath) still required considerable force to remove. <B>Note:</B> this bolt bothers me, as it only uses the altenator as a nut, and does not have a "true" locking system to prevent losening.<P>so, any thoughts, similar experience's or opinions?  am i just a idiot that doesnt know how to properly tighten a belt?  i can live with that, if that is the case. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif">  or do i have a more serious problem, such as a bad water pump, timing chain, pulley or karma?<P>thanks in advance!<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||09-26-2000||08:12 PM||||Are you sure the alternator is good? the bearings aren't dry sounding or anything like that.<P>If it's like most cars.... the bolt goes thru the alternator and the nut is part of the other arm on the bracket. Yes?<P>1. clean out the hole thru the alternator as best you can.<P>2. put antisieze on the whole lenth of the bolt. (just not the head.) that will keep it from seizing in the alternator hole.<P>I did a cave article on serpent belts. It's mostly for 4 cyl but it may help you. Seprents are very canky about tension. <P>The water pump isn't going to coast much when spun. you have the water and the seal dragging on the shaft. The seal should be pretty snug if it's worth anything.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 09-26-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||09-26-2000||08:22 PM||||oop. mixed up my bolts.... The one I'm talking about is the long one that the alternator pivots on. If that bolt is bound you'll have trouble adjusting the belt tension.<P>Your sure you mean serpent belt and not V belt? I haven't seen a V6 with a serpent but then I haven't seen any thing beyond a 86 V6.<P>The adjuster lock bolt using the laternator for a nut is normal. I can't think of any adjustable alternator that's not like that. If you are worried about it coming loose put a dab of locktight on it. (Make sure its the removable stuff!) The bolt itself should have teeth under the head. At least on most cars it does. the teeth help lock the bolt. They can wear off or someone may have lost the original bolt.||||reg||1
Z||000003||LAMBO||09-27-2000||08:23 AM||||theogre, both my 86 gts and my 85 2m6 have the serpentine belts. One v-belt drives the ac compressor. Although these v6s use a serpentine style belt, they're not used the way a serp. belt is designed to be used. Usually, something (tensioner, at least one pulley) uses the back (smooth) side of the belt. Thats one of the reasons why they are wide, to give enough friction area to actually spin something from the smooth side. I've noticed on all of my v6's that the edges of these belts start to fray after a while. Not exactly sure why, but they do. At least they are easy to change. I always carry a spare.<BR>TL  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||09-27-2000||08:52 AM||||I thought that was the case with Fiero V6 serpents but like I said I just haven't seen one yet.<P>That sounds allot like maybe one of the pulleys is out of line or they are over tight.<P>I know there's nothing about serpent at all in Haynes, but has anyone looked in the shop books to see if there is a tension spec in # or Kilo's? I do know that there is a scale you can use on V belts to adjust them. I'm not sure if the same tension scale will work on serpents. I haven't seen a belt scale in a long time.||||reg||1
Z||000005||bHooper||09-27-2000||11:25 AM||||ok, so let me ask this... since i dont have the a/c hooked up (compressor is seized, so i  am not running a belt on that), should i use a serpentine belt for a car <B>w/o</B> a/c, or keep using one like i have (for a/c)?<P>2ndly, the Clymer manual does list the ftlbs for the belt, i believe it is 100 ftlbs (new) and 80 ftlbs (after wear), i am not certain as the book is at home.<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000006||Standard||09-27-2000||01:03 PM||||It should be the same belt regardless, because the serpentine-style belt only runs the Alt and water pump, while a seperate V-belt runs the AC compressor.<P>------------------<BR>Mike -<BR>Black 86.5 GT 5 Spd, 150,000 miles and counting!       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BURNOUT.gif">    <BR>Red 84 Sport Coupe - winter donut maker||||reg||1
Z||000007||bHooper||09-27-2000||01:49 PM||||that would make sense... but then again, these are Fiero's  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>oh yea, it has been near impossible to find the proper gauge to check the ftlbs on the belts.  i have asked around at the various parts store's.<P>i think i will get this on, so i can drive it tommorow, and take it to a full service station and have them check the belt for tightness. <P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||09-27-2000||03:01 PM||||Most auto shops don't have the belt scale either. It's sort of an odd tool to see. Most mechanics don't even know what it is when they see it.<P>ft/#? read over the stuff in that book carefully. It sounds like they are sticking a torque wrench on the alternator someplace to be giving a ft/# number. That would make this pretty easy if they are.||||reg||1
Z||000009||bHooper||09-27-2000||03:47 PM||||ogre, dont forget i am going from "virtuall" memory... i will let you know for certain tonight when i can look at my clymer.<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000010||bHooper||09-29-2000||01:29 PM||||Ok, got the new belt on last night, and drove it to work this morn.  at one point (for about 20 seconds) the volts dropped and i smelt a bit of a "burning" odor, but then it corrected itself.<P>at lunch, i took it to a country mechanic to look at, but he wasnt in.  driving it around for a hour or so, everything seemed to work fine!<P>when i stopped to get gas, i checked the belt and noticed a bit of fraying, or fuzz, building up on the outside edge (closest to the battery)!  <P>so, what does this mean?... bad pulley/s?  time to replace the bearing in them?  how do i determine which one it is?<P>frustrated<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000011||theogre||09-29-2000||01:53 PM||||Voltage drop and burnt smell, belt imediatly wrecked...... either the water pump or the alternator has a problem. Likely a bearing is shot.<P>They may feel good with the belt off but once they are loaded they bind. And yes they can do this intermitantly. ||||reg||1
Z||000012||bHooper||09-30-2000||12:56 PM||||Eureka! I believe I have located and fixed the problem... it was the pulley on the altenator.  It had a wobble in the pulley.<P>I have replaced the altenator... again.  And hope that this has fixed the problem.<P><P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000013||bHooper||09-30-2000||05:21 PM||||so much for my theory.  got in the car, turned out of the driveway... same results.<P>however, i did get a good look at the pulley's and belt in motion and have come to the conclusion that the altenator pulley is the culprit.<P>where can i find a new one for the serpentine belt?<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000014||theogre||09-30-2000||09:00 PM||||didn't the new alternator come with the pulley on it? If it did then return it as defective.<P>If you had to move the pulley it's posible the original was damaged by the belt when the bearing hung up. The belt takes incredible amounts of force to break the way you said one did. That would account for it still wobling.<P>You'll likely have to go to the scrap yard to get one. They should be the same on a number of cars. the scrap yard isn't likely to let you have just a pulley.||||reg||1
Z||000015||bHooper||10-01-2000||07:58 AM||||Ogre, the new altenator's from Advanced Auto, come with V-drive pulley's.  so they have to swap that for my grooved pulley.<P><P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000016||theogre||10-01-2000||10:37 AM||||sounds like they got you the wrong part. The one I got from advance for my 87 4 cyl had the right pulley on it. It's also 105 amp job. (I always get the biggest one available... to many years jumping other peoples cars.)<P>In any case it sounds like the pulley is hozed and will have to be replaced. Problem is allot of scrape yards won't let you have just a pully. They want to unload a whole alternator.||||reg||1
Z||000017||bHooper||10-03-2000||07:18 AM||||Ah HA!  I have found the source of <B>ALL</B> my problems!  The coolant pipe that connects to the hose from the water pump, and runs along the right rear tirewell, was out of its channel and the altenator pulley was rubbing the foam insulation, causing the belt to fray!<P>I fixed the pipe, got it back in its channel.  Then replaced the pulley on the altenator (well I had done this first, anyway).  Voila, no more rubbing.<P>Unfortunatley, I had failed to get the pulley torqued tight enough, so the belt and pulley were slipping, last night.<P>Got up this AM, removed/fixed/reinstalled the altenator in 30 minutes!  Hopefully, everything will be all right now.  I am planning on driving the car to work, will know for sure then.<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000018||Old Lar||10-03-2000||07:46 AM||||I like to here the resolutions to these vexing problems.  Although too many times the fix is simple, finding the problem is the challenge.  Now we have a new place to look at  for belt problems.  Thanks for posting the discovery.||||reg||10
